WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.555
Process and persistence clues help analysts understand what ran and what was configured to run again.

00:00:07.555 --> 00:00:16.836
During live response, running processes, command lines, parent-child relationships, loaded modules, network connections, and security tool telemetry may be useful.

00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:24.822
Because live collection can change the system, analysts should document collection actions and preserve volatile evidence deliberately.

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:33.672
Windows services, scheduled tasks, startup folders, run keys, and WMI-related persistence at a high level can all be relevant.

00:00:33.672 --> 00:00:42.090
These mechanisms are used for legitimate administration and software operations, so their presence alone does not prove compromise.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:54.824
Analysts should ask whether the item fits the system role, whether it appeared during the timeline of concern, and whether related logs or file metadata support the interpretation.

00:00:54.824 --> 00:00:59.357
Suspicious binaries and script execution should be reviewed defensively.

00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:13.819
Useful questions include where the file came from, when it appeared, which account interacted with it, whether it matches known software, whether security tools recorded activity, and whether logs show related execution.

00:01:13.819 --> 00:01:20.942
Hashes, metadata, signatures, path analysis, and EDR or antivirus telemetry can all help with triage.

00:01:20.942 --> 00:01:24.611
The safest forensic posture is careful correlation.

00:01:24.611 --> 00:01:32.166
A service entry, scheduled task, script, or detection alert may be a lead, not a conclusion.

00:01:32.166 --> 00:01:40.152
Malware triage and persistence analysis should protect evidence, avoid unnecessary execution, and stay inside authorized response procedures.

00:01:40.152 --> 00:01:46.843
The analyst's job is to explain what the evidence supports and what remains uncertain.
